# Aetherfield — the canonical list of environment variables.
#
# NAMES ONLY. Real values never land in this file or in the repository; they
# come from `vercel env pull .env.local`, which is gitignored (AGENTS.md 8.4).
#
# Every variable here is server-only. Phase one needs no `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` at
# all, and adding one is a decision to make a value public.
#
# This file is extended by the build step that provisions each variable — never
# ahead of it, because the provider owns the name and it is read back from
# `vercel env ls` rather than guessed.

# --- Step 1: Neon Postgres (provisioned) -----------------------------------
# WHICH BRANCH EACH VALUE POINTS AT (prompt 90, 16 Aug 2026):
#   Vercel Production            -> the Neon `main` branch, the default one.
#   Vercel Preview               -> `preview/<git-branch>`, created per
#                                   deployment and NOT readable from here.
#   Your local `.env.local`      -> the Neon `development` branch.
# Local development and the whole E2E matrix run against `development`, so that
# `npm run dev` and `npm run test:e2e` cannot read or write production rows.
#
# The two names below are still ONE integration-managed row each, spanning all
# three environments, and prompt 90 deliberately did not split them. Preview is
# isolated by a different mechanism: Neon's native preview branching, enabled on
# the Vercel-side resource. Vercel webhooks Neon at deployment time, Neon clones
# `main`, and the branch's connection variables are injected into that build
# ALONE, overriding the row below for that deployment only. Neon's docs are
# explicit that those injected values "cannot be accessed or viewed in your
# Vercel project's environment variable settings" — so there is no Preview value
# to read back here, and its absence from `vercel env ls` is correct, not a gap.
#
# A preview branch is a FULL clone of `main`, not a schema-only one, so it
# carries production's rows — including real applications and CV references.
# That is a known, accepted exposure; see docs/backend.md for the reasoning and
# the pruning obligation.
#
# Repointing a local checkout is a manual edit of the two names below — there is
# no script, and no value ever appears in this file or in the repository. See
# docs/backend.md, "A development database branch, prompt 89" and "Preview
# deployments get their own database branch, prompt 90".
#
# Pooled (PgBouncer, the `-pooler` host). What the application uses, and the
# only one `lib/db/client.ts` reads.
DATABASE_URL=
# Direct. What migrations, pg_dump, logical replication and LISTEN/NOTIFY
# require, and what `drizzle.config.ts` reads. The app never uses it.
DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED=
# Optional, local tooling only — NOT an application variable, which is why it
# has no `=` line here and is not set on Vercel. `neonctl` reads `NEON_API_KEY`
# to list, create and address Neon branches; nothing in `app/` or `lib/` reads
# it, and the app runs without it. Mint one from the Neon console reached by
# `vercel integration open neon neon-purple-candle` (Vercel SSO — the resource
# was provisioned `--no-claim`, so a personal Neon login does not see it).
# NEON_API_KEY

# --- Step 2: Upstash Redis (provisioned) -----------------------------------
# The REST endpoint and its token, under the names the Upstash Marketplace
# integration actually sets — `KV_REST_API_*`, not the `UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_*`
# that Upstash's own docs and `Redis.fromEnv()` expect. Read back from
# `vercel env ls`, not guessed. `lib/rate-limit/` reads them, lazily, so
# `next build` succeeds without them. Neither is NEXT_PUBLIC_*: a rate limiter
# whose token a browser can read is not a rate limiter.
KV_REST_API_URL=
KV_REST_API_TOKEN=
# Also set by the integration and deliberately unused: KV_REST_API_READ_ONLY_TOKEN
# (a limiter counts, so it needs the write token), KV_URL and REDIS_URL (the
# TCP endpoints; the REST client is what runs on Fluid Compute).
# BotID (build step 2) needs no environment variable at all — the challenge is
# proxied through the app's own origin by `withBotId` in `next.config.ts`.

# --- Step 3: Resend ---------------------------------------------------------
# The Resend API key. `lib/email/send.ts` reads it, lazily, so `next build`
# succeeds without it. Never NEXT_PUBLIC_*: the Resend API has no CORS support
# precisely because a browser-readable key is a key anyone can send mail with.
#
# NOT provisioned through the Vercel Marketplace, and that is a recorded
# deviation from AGENTS.md 7.4 rather than an oversight —
# `vercel integration add resend` requires `-m domain=<a domain you own>` and
# Aetherfield has none. See docs/backend.md, step 3.
RESEND_API_KEY=
# Where a demo request's internal notification goes. Ours, not the provider's,
# so the name is chosen here. Server-only: an internal address in a browser
# bundle is a harvestable spam target. Unset is supported — the notification is
# skipped with a log line naming no address.
LEAD_NOTIFICATION_EMAIL=

# --- Step 5: Vercel Blob (provisioned) --------------------------------------
# The read-write token for the `aetherfield-cv` store, created private, in
# iad1, and connected to production, preview and development. `lib/storage/cv.ts`
# reads it, lazily, so `next build` succeeds without it. Never NEXT_PUBLIC_*:
# this token can write to and read from the store, and a CV is personal data
# (AGENTS.md 8.3). The name was read back from `vercel env ls`, not guessed.
#
# Vercel Blob is first-party, not a Marketplace integration, so AGENTS.md 7.4's
# `vercel integration add` procedure did not apply. The store was created with
#   vercel blob create-store aetherfield-cv --access private --region iad1
# and the subcommand name was read from `vercel blob --help` — it is
# `create-store`, not the `store add` prompts/48 predicted.
BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=
# Where a job application's internal notification goes. Ours, not the
# provider's, so the name is chosen here. Server-only: a recruiting address in a
# browser bundle is a harvestable spam target. Unset is supported — the
# notification is skipped with a log line naming no address, exactly
# LEAD_NOTIFICATION_EMAIL's contract.
#
# A separate variable rather than a reuse of that one: applications go to a
# recruiting inbox and demo requests to a sales one, and collapsing the two is a
# decision nobody has made.
APPLICATION_NOTIFICATION_EMAIL=

# --- Step 6: Better Auth ---------------------------------------------------
# Generated locally, at least 32 characters. Rotation uses
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRETS (plural), which is not configured until it is needed.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
# The application's base URL. Server-only; callbacks resolve against it.
#
# Step 4 gave it a second reader and deliberately did NOT add a variable for it:
# `appBaseUrl()` in lib/email/config.ts resolves the absolute confirm and
# unsubscribe links in the newsletter's emails against this value. Inventing an
# APP_URL alongside it would be a second name for one fact and one more way for
# two parts of the app to disagree about where they live (AGENTS.md 8.4: do not
# invent a variable name). It throws when unset rather than sending a link to
# `undefined/newsletter/confirm`.
BETTER_AUTH_URL=
# Google OAuth Web application credentials. The configured redirect URI must
# be <BETTER_AUTH_URL>/api/auth/callback/google for each environment.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- Step 14: the nightly sweep --------------------------------------------
# Generated locally, like BETTER_AUTH_SECRET, and NOT auto-provisioned — Vercel
# does not set this one; the developer does. It authenticates the only caller of
# /api/cron/recalculate, which recalculates every tenant's emissions and raises
# threshold alerts, so an unset value fails CLOSED rather than open.
#
# Added with `vercel env add CRON_SECRET <env>` and the name read back from
# `vercel env ls`, not guessed. Sensitive on production and preview; the CLI
# refuses --sensitive on development, so it is stored non-sensitive there.
#
# Never NEXT_PUBLIC_*: a cron secret in a browser bundle is the secret given
# away, and phase two still adds no public variable at all.
CRON_SECRET=
